    "description": "The Payments API accepts authorization requests from merchant webhooks while the Settlement Service processes them asynchronously. HTTP handlers must return an acknowledgement quickly, but a burst of retries can exceed settlement throughput. Introduce a hand-off channel with a hard capacity of 5,000 messages: producers enqueue without waiting for processing, consumers remove messages independently, and producers block for at most 50 ms when the channel is full before returning HTTP 429. Twenty settlement workers share the channel, and each message carries an idempotency key so retries remain safe. During shutdown, producers stop accepting work, consumers finish already queued messages for up to 30 seconds, and unfinished messages are moved to durable storage. The key decision is how to decouple request arrival from processing rate while ensuring overload creates visible backpressure rather than an unbounded memory backlog.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the bounded handoff and overload behavior that decouple request producers from settlement consumers without allowing the backlog to grow without limit.",
    "notes": "Producer-consumer fits the bounded 5000-message channel decoupling webhook producers from settlement consumers with backpressure on exhaustion; thread-pool is the near-miss (shared worker pool over the queue) and counting-semaphore supports the capacity bound."
  },
